<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Šabac</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Szabács</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Šabac is a city in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sava">Sava</a> river. Its fortress was built by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> in 1471, taken by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matthias-corvinus">Matthias Corvinus</a> in 1476 and held by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> as part of its southern defences until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suleiman-the-magnificent">Suleiman the Magnificent</a> captured it, together with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgrade">Belgrade</a>, in 1521. In 1806 the nearby <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-misar">Battle of Mišar</a> was an early Serbian victory over the Ottomans in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-serbian-uprising">First Serbian Uprising</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sabac.jpg" alt="Šabac" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ванилица · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Zgrada_Trijumf,_%C5%A0abac_001.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
